00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.680
So let's spend a
few minutes talking

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.950
a little bit about serverless.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.710
As I go spend time
with many developers,

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:58.040
they are adopting serverless
at their own pace.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.680
But there are key
entities from serverless,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.540
which make it very attractive.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.000
The first one is the aspect
of completely abstracting away

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.380
the underlying infrastructure.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.470
So developers love to code.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.330
And they want to really
just deal with code

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.310
and say, here's my code.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.630
Just go make it happen.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.322
Just go run it for me.

00:02:20.322 --> 00:02:22.280
Obviously, there's some
other business benefits

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.970
to be ascertained from that
as well, such as agility.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.550
Because you're working
with code as a construct,

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.640
and you are not worrying
about infrastructure,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.970
assuming a provider can manage
that for you, you become more

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.430
faster rolling out
capabilities, which

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.150
are more differentiated
for your business,

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.370
and gain competitive advantage.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.860
Productivity is another
key aspect of it.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.630
Because you can do
that faster, you

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.330
have multiple teams who
can collaborate and build

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.800
capabilities at a
much faster pace.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.550
So serverless is
obviously catching up

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.580
as you saw today
with a show of hands.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.250
A lot of folks are
looking at serverless

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.770
as the next style
of architecture

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:06.850
in building newer
modern capabilities.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.460
And microservices kind
of goes hand in hand

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.290
with serverless in a big way.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.260
Usually when folks are
refactoring the applications

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.800
from monolith into smaller
microservices, many of them

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.400
are choosing serverless as
the key architecture style

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.460
to build those applications.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:29.110
And clearly, we have seen a
big evolution in serverless.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.910
When serverless here the
stage a few years back,

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.060
it was all about
functions, or also known

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.250
as functions as a service.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:39.820
Clearly, there's been an
evolution from functions

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:46.460
to building full-blown apps, and
now to serverless containers.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.440
Of course, Google Cloud has
capabilities no matter which

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.670
serverless flavor you pick.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.040
But this brings a
very important element

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.440
of choice, which
is no matter what

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.650
style you pick, you have
the ability in Google Cloud

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:04.460
today to be able to
leverage the specific flavor

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:08.300
and create application to
power some awesome experiences.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.460
Just like the
internet happened, we

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:19.089
believe that developers
have a choice.

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:24.070
They're going to use
various frameworks for very

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.500
obvious productivity gains.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.220
And so trying to control
which framework they use

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.810
doesn't really work out.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.580
Because they have
a choice, you want

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:40.110
to empower developers and make
sure they have all the access

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.080
and controls to be able to
use a framework of choice.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.890
But of course, as
an organization,

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:48.120
you also want to be
able to put some method,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.520
put some control around it, so
you can manage it very well.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.300
But the reality is developers
are using many underlying

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.300
frameworks-- some of these
which are mentioned here--

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.960
to build new
capabilities, but more so

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.500
are increasingly doing
that on different clouds.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.990
Just like yourselves,
many customers

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.900
are choosing different
clouds for various reasons.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.010
It could be diversifying risk.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.430
It could be some cloud has
some capabilities better

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.620
than the others, right?

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.880
What I want to do now is kind
of poke a little bit deeper

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:27.210
into what we believe are
some of the key differences

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.860
between a service.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.460
So at the end of
the day, when you're

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.460
building a serverless
application,

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.990
you are delivering a service--

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.630
service of some
value to something.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.110
So let's say it's
a piece of code,

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:45.660
a snippet, or the entire runtime
as we just saw in a container--

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.220
the other spectrum of
the serverless flavor.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.670
Let's say you write
something in there.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.590
Let's pick an example
of an ordering system.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:54.090
OK.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.150
So you have a small service
that can take orders.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.300
That microservice is
an architecture style.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.520
That's your backend,
if you would.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.120
That's what you are writing
in terms of business logic,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.430
as your code as a developer.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:20.080
Now, the moment that service
gets pushed out to a consumer--

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.790
so someone who starts
to access that service--

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.370
that becomes an API.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:26.630
So an API is a contract.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:31.460
This is the front-end of how
a service is usually accessed.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:33.800
In many instances,
these two concepts

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.050
are assumed to be
implicitly together.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.790
So as an example,
when you roll out

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.220
some piece of serverless
capability, of course

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.710
it has an endpoint.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:44.660
But there's a clean
line of separation.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:48.950
And we'll explain why
it is the way it is.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.980
As you start to examine
how folks are refactoring

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.240
into a newer modern
applications,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.020
well, it turns out there were
legacy applications on one

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.763
of the applications
which existed before.

00:06:59.763 --> 00:07:01.680
It turns out they were
consumers who are still

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.803
using those applications.

00:07:03.803 --> 00:07:05.220
So those consumers
are still using

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.370
an API, which is a contract
into an application.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.870
The difference is
now you've kind

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:14.470
of migrated or to new serverless
style of building applications.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:16.450
So your backend
has really changed.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:17.910
It's very important
to understand

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:22.650
the semantic difference
between a service and an API.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.420
And we'll touch on
that a little bit more.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.410
So why does that matter?

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:30.380
At the end of the
day, enterprises

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.230
are building
serverless applications

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:37.220
to eventually power
digital experiences.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.550
You heard Thomas
talk this morning

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:43.070
about digital transformation.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.840
What does digital
transformation really mean?

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.670
It means that in
this day and age,

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:53.270
enterprises are building
digital products without nothing

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:55.190
but APIs.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:58.760
So API has become
the new mechanism

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:02.690
to express capabilities
for businesses.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:06.500
And these APIs, which
power these connected data

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.080
experiences-- whether
that is powering

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.840
an app that is used
by your employees,

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.320
or a third-party partner,
or essentially building

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.150
an ecosystem of
different experiences,

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:18.820
it are these APIs
that really start

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.280
to take the value that
you're building in serverless

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:25.020
and make it accessible
to consumers.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.120
So let's take a step
up and say, well great,

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.980
I have built some capabilities.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.620
How do I really manage this?

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.429
Turns out the moment you build
some stuff, which can easily

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:40.590
be accessed, the very
first thing and you

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.860
start thinking about
is security, right?

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.670
Is this serverless capability
that I built, is it

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.170
being accessed by
someone who really

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.540
has the rights to
access, or is authorized

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.550
to access that capability?

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:54.270
Right?

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:59.730
Can I control it from making
sure that it is not abused?

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.430
Of course, serverless is going
to give you endless scalability

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.410
behind the scenes for you.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.840
But you still want to be able
to ensure what if there's

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:08.370
a business quota to it?

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.500
What if you have signed
up for the service,

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.120
and you're really
only allowed to use

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.710
that 1,000 times a minute?

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:18.800
Because maybe I'm offering
a different tier or service,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.950
and one partner gets more
fair share than the other,

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:24.777
based on a subscription plan.

00:09:24.777 --> 00:09:26.360
That the other aspect
that we get into

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:30.590
is, great, how do developers
and consumers of serverless--

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:32.030
how do they really
find out about

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:35.210
your serverless applications?

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:39.040
Is there a mechanism for them
to discover them somewhere?

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.260
And lastly, for the enterprise,
it's also about great,

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:48.340
we took this big effort of
refactoring our monolith

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:53.590
and legacy applications into
modern, serverless style apps.

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.550
How do we know people
are using them?

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:57.780
Is there an adoption?

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:00.420
What is my ROI?

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.240
These are some very
hard questions,

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.840
which technical teams have
to answer to the businesses,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.840
because someone is making
an investment in that.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:15.780
Turns out there is a way
to answer these questions.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.820
So an API management
platform brings a lot

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:23.430
of these capabilities
out of the box,

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:27.850
so you as enterprises
can continue

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:31.570
focusing on building
business logic,

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:37.120
writing code in serverless
style development paradigms,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.920
yet have the peace of mind
around how you can use an API

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.360
management platform--

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.940
like Apigee Edge-- to be
able to control access

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:53.760
to your servers apps, provide
the right level of security,

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.970
also provide the ability for
your serverless applications

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:01.560
to be discovered easily, so
that application developers who

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.600
are consuming our
serverless apps

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:05.640
can very quickly
connect with them,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:09.000
work with them, and then more
importantly as an enterprise,

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.340
use some key dashboards, which
give you a sense of adoption

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.430
metrics, operational metrics
because all those things are

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.690
very important.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:19.190
OK.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:27.040
So now let's take a
slightly deeper dive

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.840
into what does API management
for serverless actually mean.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.770
For today, in this
session, we are

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.710
going to very quickly
demonstrate some real world

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:42.060
capabilities using Google
Cloud technologies, including

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:43.650
Apigee Edge.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.260
But let's spend a couple
of minutes on this slide.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.070
So it turns out
when developers take

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:53.800
on decomposing a big
monolith application,

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.650
the goal is to be able
to separate them out

00:11:56.650 --> 00:12:00.140
into discrete and
independent usable serverless

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:02.500
apps or chunks.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.720
Which means there's
always a need

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.950
to take a bunch of these
serverless applications

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:13.050
and compose them into
something more meaningful.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:16.670
So I spoke very briefly about
the connected experiences

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:18.470
that you're trying to deliver.

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:19.970
Ultimately, whatever
you're building

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:26.070
the serverless is powering
some experience to a consumer.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:30.470
So turns out there are multiple
ways and multiple design

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:33.620
patterns that you need to
be able to adopt to access

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.060
these capabilities,
which are now

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:38.790
scattered across in
multiple functions, or apps,

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.000
or even containers.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:42.290
So an API gateway--

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:44.570
which is part of the API
management platform--

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:48.530
starts to take a very central
role in giving you the tools

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:52.910
and widgets to be able to not
only protect your serverless

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.950
applications, but
more importantly,

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:59.960
take data from several
of them, compose them

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:03.650
into a higher level experience
API, and deliver that.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:06.920
And in doing that, you can
use a lot of capability

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.490
from the API management
platform such as ensuring

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:14.170
the right level of security,
right level of access,

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:17.630
and the ability for you to make
some transformations for them.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:18.340
OK.

