to talk about this kind of
from the practitioner level.

00:13:50.423 --> 00:13:52.590
What's happening now, I
guess, if you strip away all

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:56.250
the buzzwords-- serverless,
multi-cloud, all the noise--

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.470
you just have more
tools to build whatever

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:01.600
you need to build and ship it.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:02.850
So we'll start with Cloud Run.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.400
It's kind of this
serverless runtime

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:06.180
we have from Google Cloud.

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.220
My foray into the
serverless world,

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:10.350
I liked the idea of these
functions and these code

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:11.290
snippets.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.832
But there are some
times where I just

00:14:12.832 --> 00:14:15.752
really need to write the app in
a way that I'm familiar with.

00:14:15.752 --> 00:14:16.960
Some of the frameworks helps.

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:18.267
Sometimes it doesn't.

00:14:18.267 --> 00:14:20.100
So in the case of Cloud
Run, I can basically

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:23.910
build an app in any language I
want, build a Docker container,

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:26.102
push it to a registry,
and then deploy it.

00:14:26.102 --> 00:14:27.810
If you've never seen
Cloud Run in action,

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.167
I'll show you a little bit now.

00:14:29.167 --> 00:14:31.500
So I have a couple of things
that we're going to deploy.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.700
We're going to step
to this one by one.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.760
So Cloud Run is really
straightforward.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.640
What you have here's a
very simple application.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.658
Let's do this.

00:14:40.658 --> 00:14:41.700
So this is just a go app.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:43.080
It's really simple.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.145
And the goal of this
whole serverless paradigm

00:14:45.145 --> 00:14:47.520
is you're supposed to focus
on the business logic, right?

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:50.758
So you're not going to have
a lot of things like retries.

00:14:50.758 --> 00:14:52.800
You're not going to do
any of your typical things

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.000
that you would do with Nginx.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:54.960
You're not going
to put that here.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.335
You're going to just kind of
focus on the business logic.

00:14:57.335 --> 00:14:59.850
Today, my business logic is
just reporting the runtime

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.000
that I'm using.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.000
I want you to focus on
the overall architecture,

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.440
not necessarily what
this app is doing.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.140
So when I deployed
this container,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:11.460
it's just going to
respond Cloud Run.

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:13.800
The reason why I like this
particular paradigm is I

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.020
like to be able to develop
locally on my laptop

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.620
using common tools.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:20.130
So if I say go build,
what I'm expecting

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:23.610
is that I can actually test
this particular runtime

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.320
on my local machine.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.840
And if I wanted to, I should
be able to hit this with Curl

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:32.010
using the familiar process
that I've been using for years.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:35.130
Most of this stuff is data
comes in, data goes out.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:37.110
It shouldn't be
that complicated.

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:39.090
You can see here that
I hit it with Curl,

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:41.100
and it says Cloud Run.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.360
This could be any
runtime, any language.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:44.350
We don't care.

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:46.140
It's just going to
make some system calls.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.640
You're going to get
an HTTP request,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.960
and you're going to respond.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:54.780
So the way we deploy this
is with a Docker file.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.565
So if you've used Docker
before, a very similar pattern.

00:15:57.565 --> 00:15:58.940
I don't care what
you doing here.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.760
You're going to end up with some
container image with some entry

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:02.420
point.

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:04.070
I'm using a two-step
build just to make

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:06.080
this container super small.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:08.600
There's a lot of people in
the serverless community that

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:11.770
hear containers, and then
instantly just like catch fire.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:15.590
I'm like, it's a
packaging format.

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:17.490
You can ship source code.

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:19.280
You can ship a binary.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:20.870
And this is just
another mechanism

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:22.940
for shipping a binary.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.160
So if you look here
in my two-part build,

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:28.550
the only thing I'm shipping
is this small binary here.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:30.390
And I'm throwing
away everything else.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:31.557
OK.

00:16:31.557 --> 00:16:33.140
So this just gives
me that flexibility

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:35.942
of deploying what I want
in my own container image.

00:16:35.942 --> 00:16:38.400
Then you ask yourself, how do
I actually deploy this thing?

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.317
So you can build this
thing using Cloud Build.

00:16:40.317 --> 00:16:43.310
I don't actually have Docker
installed on my laptop anymore

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:47.870
these days because I use a
tool we call Cloud Build.

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.203
And what I can do is I
can submit my source code

00:16:50.203 --> 00:16:52.370
and that Docker file, and
it will just build it here

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:53.990
from my laptop.

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.790
Or I can use the CI/CD flow,
and it will just do it for me.

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:59.850
But the idea is we want to make
this as simple as possible.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:02.400
Some of our IDE plugins, this
will be automate it for you.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:05.000
So you hit Save,
it will do a build.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:08.400
And then how do you deploy
it to Cloud Run itself?

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.000
We also want that to
be straightforward.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.687
So if you look at my
display command, that's it.

00:17:13.687 --> 00:17:15.520
The nice thing about
this particular pattern

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:17.859
is if you already have
a container running

00:17:17.859 --> 00:17:20.151
on Docker, or on
Kubernetes, and you

00:17:20.151 --> 00:17:21.609
want to get rid of
the cluster, you

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:23.670
want to get rid of
the infrastructure,

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:25.270
you just run this one command.

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.367
Tell us what the image
is, and we'll deploy it

00:17:27.367 --> 00:17:29.200
and run it for you in
that serverless style.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:31.990
We scale to zero, and we
scale up on the request

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:33.093
when it comes.

00:17:33.093 --> 00:17:35.260
So when we run this command,
what you end up getting

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:35.998
is a URL.

00:17:35.998 --> 00:17:37.040
So we'll click over here.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.120
So this is what the
Cloud Run URL looks like.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:42.912
We also mentioned in the keynote
earlier that in Cloud Run,

00:17:42.912 --> 00:17:44.870
you can also bring your
own Kubernetes cluster.

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.330
So if you're on-prem--

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.932
maybe you're already using GKE.

00:17:47.932 --> 00:17:49.390
You have a collection
of workloads,

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:52.000
and you just want to use that
serverless style, that service

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:52.810
pattern--

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:54.280
you can run these side by side.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:57.860
You can actually say, G Cloud,
deploy to this GKE cluster,

00:17:57.860 --> 00:17:59.560
and it will show up
in the same console.

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:01.690
So you can kind mix
and match if you really

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:04.690
need access to like a GPU
or some specialized hardware

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:07.810
that we don't do on the
stateless service platform

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:08.477
here.

00:18:08.477 --> 00:18:09.310
So here's our thing.

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:10.900
It's called Runtime.

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.480
And it comes with the URL
and Talis certificate.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.580
You can also map a
custom domain to it.

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:17.577
But we're just going to
keep it really simple.

00:18:17.577 --> 00:18:19.660
One thing that we've done
is you don't necessarily

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:22.330
need to use an API
gateway day one.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:25.190
You can just hit this
particular endpoint.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.230
And in my case, this
is a private function.

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:30.280
So if you authenticate to
it, then you'll be charged.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.770
If you do not authenticate
to this endpoint,

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:35.840
you won't be charged, and your
function will never be invoked.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:36.340
OK.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.462
So that's an
important thing here.

00:18:38.462 --> 00:18:39.670
So we'll copy and paste this.

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:41.628
I'm just going to run
Curl on my local machine.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.910
And we get this 403.

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:49.160
And the reason why is that
I don't want people hitting

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.240
this container directly.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:53.300
I actually want to go through
some proxy or some gateway.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:55.730
And this is where this
Apigee piece comes in.

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:57.620
As a developer, what
I want to focus on

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.270
is just that business
logic, and then

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:01.760
deploy my endpoints
wherever that may be.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:03.630
So the first I want
to do is say, hey,

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:06.170
instead of me configure
authentication here in my app,

00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:09.770
we're going to delegate
that to Apigee.

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:11.607
Now, I'm pretty new
to Apigee myself.

00:19:11.607 --> 00:19:13.190
So last year, I built
a bunch of hacks

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:18.410
to create OAuth tokens to store
in Apigee's key value store,

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:21.220
and then tell Apigee to pull
it out, put it in a header,

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:22.570
called my code.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:24.742
I was like, this is terrible.

00:19:24.742 --> 00:19:25.700
I call the Apigee team.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:27.950
I was like, there's no way
we work at the same company

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:29.780
and I have to do these
kind of workarounds.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.240
So they built some
extensions for me.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.680
I was like, let me see them.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:34.595
Do they work?

00:19:34.595 --> 00:19:36.470
That's what you always
ask your product team.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:37.808
Does it work?

00:19:37.808 --> 00:19:39.350
They always say yeah
for some reason.

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:44.800
So to think about
this is there's

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:46.897
a new set of things
called extensions.

00:19:46.897 --> 00:19:48.980
And what you do is you
find one of the extensions.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:51.130
So in this case, I want
basically our Google auth

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:52.210
extension.

00:19:52.210 --> 00:19:55.340
And what the Google
auth the extension does

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.290
is the Google auth
extension will

00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:01.220
allow me to use OAuth token or
admit one for me automatically.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:04.340
Let me go through this.

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:07.360
This is off.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:08.890
So here I just
called it Cloud Run,

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:11.430
but it's using the Google
authentication extension.

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:13.090
And what this does
is I can configured

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:16.210
this particular extension
with a service account,

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:18.800
and tell it what it's
going to be used for.

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.112
So if you click here,
you'll be given the ability

00:20:21.112 --> 00:20:23.320
to configure this and deploy
it to one of your Apigee

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:24.173
environments.

00:20:24.173 --> 00:20:25.840
So I basically have
a set of credentials

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:27.760
that we don't show
you on the screen.

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.580
I'm glad because
that would be bad.

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:33.220
And then basically at this
point this particular extension

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:34.720
using that service
account can then

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:36.250
be referenced when
I create a proxy.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:37.660
So if you're new to Apigee--

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:38.690
like I am--

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:41.273
I'm going to walk you through a
little bit of what this means.

00:20:41.273 --> 00:20:43.420
So the first step is
we use this extension.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:45.820
It will start to create
OAuth tokens for me

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:47.890
automatically behind the scenes.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:50.110
And I also have to
give access in IAM

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:52.120
that it can actually
invoke certain functions

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:53.470
or cloud run instances.

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:56.050
So it's not going to be on a
calling thing out of the box.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:21:00.150
You still have IAM control
over what it can and can't do.

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:02.920
Now in the Apigee space, we
have to create these proxies.

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.440
So the first one we're going
to create this Cloud Run proxy.

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:08.750
And this is enterprise
stuff, so we're

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:11.465
using XML for configuration.

00:21:11.465 --> 00:21:13.090
I guess that makes
it enterprise-ready.

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:16.190
So you see all this XML.

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:18.680
Don't die.

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:19.340
I almost did.

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:21.630
I had to come back to life.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:24.000
So the first thing
here is this extension.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.330
So the nice thing, though,
is there is a UI Composer.

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:29.185
You don't really have to
write all XML by hand.

00:21:29.185 --> 00:21:30.560
So as you click
on this composer,

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.300
you can start to configure
some of these things.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:34.280
So these things were
set up ahead of time.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:35.465
Let me close this out.

00:21:35.465 --> 00:21:37.340
And what you'll see here
is the configuration

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:38.570
for this extension.

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:40.790
I'm saying I would like to
use the Google Cloud Run

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:43.623
extension using the Google
authentication backend.

00:21:43.623 --> 00:21:45.290
And then you give it
a couple of things.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:46.850
So I know from
the documentation,

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:49.490
in order to call I
can't run service,

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:51.550
I had to give it this
particular audience.

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.300
So I know behind the
scenes, the extension

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:57.050
is going to use a Google
API, admit the OAuth token

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:58.635
using that particular audience.

00:21:58.635 --> 00:22:00.260
And then it's going
to have the ability

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:02.780
to make that credential
available in this output

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:04.250
string.

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:07.160
So if you can see here, we
have this output string.

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:10.310
And this allows
me to then compose

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:13.567
a call that will extract that
out and set it into the header.

00:22:13.567 --> 00:22:15.900
I'm going to show you that
configuration really quickly,

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.250
and then we're going
to invoke this.

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:20.790
So what I'm saying now in
this particular extension

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:23.610
is I want to set in
header before you actually

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:24.300
call my app.

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:29.970
You can actually see this
visual request preset of steps.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.310
I can go to the authorization
header, reference

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:34.090
that Google Cloud
Run credential,

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.090
and it's going to inject
it in the header for me

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:37.000
automatically.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:37.895
OK.

00:22:37.895 --> 00:22:39.270
So the nice thing
is-- so this is

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.370
the thing that makes
Apigee dope in my opinion,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:43.320
this whole little trace thing.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.450
I was like, so I can
do that with Nginx.

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:47.710
Why are you showing
me this right now?

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:49.380
And then he did this.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:51.120
He clicked on the
start trace thing,

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.460
and you can actually see
this complex configuration

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:54.655
come to life.

00:22:54.655 --> 00:22:56.280
So if I hit this,
you can see that it's

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:58.980
going to actually move
through each of those steps,

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:02.500
and it's going to invoke
my particular function.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:03.190
So you see here.

00:23:03.190 --> 00:23:05.160
I'm going to walk through
what the output is.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.045
It goes out to the extension.

00:23:07.045 --> 00:23:08.670
I'm going to show
you some credentials.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.070
Don't be hacking my service
while I'm on stage, OK.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:13.140
Wait till later.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.310
If you're on YouTube, this
won't work at this point.

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:18.690
This will be expired
by time you watch this.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:19.757
All right.

00:23:19.757 --> 00:23:21.840
Let me see if I can make
this a little bit bigger.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:27.190
So down here, we're going
to have a bit of response

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:28.070
stuff happening here.

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:29.580
So this is actually going to--

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:31.100
I know its small the read here.

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:33.420
It's going to create
that OAuth token.

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:34.920
And it's going to
stored it in a way

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:38.960
that I can reference it
here when I set the header.

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:41.317
Let's move this back some.

00:23:41.317 --> 00:23:42.150
Can I collapse this?

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:43.700
There we go.

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:45.635
All right.

00:23:45.635 --> 00:23:47.260
We should really make
that-- all right.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:47.885
So there we go.

00:23:47.885 --> 00:23:49.410
So we have this access token.

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:51.960
So that access token is
going to be extracted out,

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:53.580
put it into a header,
and it's going

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:57.120
to be used to call the
service underneath.

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:58.640
And then you can
see here that it's

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:01.580
going to respond Cloud Run
just like on my local laptop.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:05.790
So if I grab this, now
hitting the Apigee endpoint,

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:09.090
we can see that we hit
our particular backend.

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:09.840
OK.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:13.740
So then what makes
Apigee relevant to having

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:15.040
multiple applications?

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:17.640
So let's try something
with Cloud Functions.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:21.338
So this is using
Cloud Run, but we also

00:24:21.338 --> 00:24:23.130
want to deploy something
to Cloud Function.

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:24.630
So if you never use
Cloud Functions,

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:26.890
this is a different take on
this whole service thing.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:29.370
This is more of a
function as a service.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:34.040
And Cloud Functions, you write
code that looks like this.

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:36.570
So you get rid of all the
server initialization,

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:38.590
and you just write your handler.

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:40.270
So this is also Go lang.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.220
We just write a normal
Go HTTP handler.

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:45.432
You'll get your
request automatically.

00:24:45.432 --> 00:24:47.140
And just like Cloud
Run, we also give you

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:49.120
an HTTP endpoint you can
hit without setting up

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:50.560
an API gateway.

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:52.870
But in this case, I also
want to make this also

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:54.300
a private function.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:56.740
Now, this is deployed
using a different thing.

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:58.558
You don't build a
container first.

00:24:58.558 --> 00:25:00.100
Here, you just use
a display command,

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:02.480
and it looks like this.

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:04.473
So I'm going to just be
in my source directory.

00:25:04.473 --> 00:25:06.640
I am going to configure
this particular thing to run

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:08.120
as a specific service account.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:09.620
And if you look
down here, I'm going

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:12.130
to grant the Apigee
services account the ability

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:13.550
to invoke this function.

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:14.050
All right.

00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:15.710
So that's critical.

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:18.320
So the nice thing too
about Apigee extensions,

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:20.770
we also have an extension
just for Cloud Functions.

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:22.900
So all you have to do is
give it the particular set

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:24.940
of credentials, and
you tell the function

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:26.410
that you want to
have it invoked.

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:28.118
And you can skip a
lot of the boilerplate

00:25:28.118 --> 00:25:29.680
that I did a little bit earlier.

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:33.157
But here's where I think
Apigee gets super powerful.

00:25:33.157 --> 00:25:35.740
The other thing you might want
to do with APIs is each of them

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:36.880
will have some response.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:38.200
Clients will hit your endpoint.

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:39.810
You'll respond however you want.

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:42.310
But there are cases where I see
people that are refactoring.

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:45.970
Maybe it's a monolith, and you
want to add a v0.2 endpoint.

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:48.102
In that case, sometimes
I'll build a small function

00:25:48.102 --> 00:25:49.060
that adds a little bit.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:51.820
And we can enhance the
particular response.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:53.650
What you can do an
Apigee is I can actually

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:56.930
compose the response before
it goes back to the client.

00:25:56.930 --> 00:25:58.390
So think about it this way.

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:00.080
I can deploy a Cloud Function.

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:02.350
It's going to have a
owns its own response.

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:03.880
I can take both
of those responses

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:06.040
and munge them into
their own output

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:08.230
before I send it
back to the client.

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:11.060
So we can walk you through that
configuration really quickly.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.150
So we have another API proxy.

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:15.280
And this one's going
to be called Mashup.

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:18.910
So the endpoint here is going to
be this Cloud Function, Mashup.

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:20.710
Then we're going to
do here is we're going

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:21.923
to look at the deploy step.

00:26:21.923 --> 00:26:24.340
And I have a bunch of these
steps that I have put in here.

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:26.230
So very similar
to the beginning,

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:29.650
we're going to use the extension
to grab the authentication

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:32.400
to call my first
service on Cloud Run.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:34.580
And it's going to respond
with this runtime.

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:36.580
And then we're going to
hit the Lambda runtime--

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:40.450
or not Lambda runtime, the
Cloud Functions runtime.

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:43.290
And that extension is going to
call it the Cloud Functions.

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:45.290
And here, I'm just going
to tell it what region.

00:26:45.290 --> 00:26:47.343
The name of the function
is also called Runtime.

00:26:47.343 --> 00:26:49.010
And then I'm going
to take the response.

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:51.460
I'm going to show you
the configuration here.

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:53.510
So in this
configuration, you can

00:26:53.510 --> 00:26:55.260
see that I'm going to
be able to reference

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:59.040
the response from the
two invocations previous.

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:00.320
So I call Cloud Run.

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:01.950
I'm going to take that output.

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:04.480
When I call Cloud Functions,
I'm going to take that output.

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:08.010
And here, instead of returning
a raw string to the client,

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:11.760
I'm going to return this JSON
payload that I'm composing here

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:13.410
without writing
any additional code

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:15.420
or hitting any other backend.

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:19.050
So what should happen here is
if I were to hit this endpoint--

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:20.970
So we'll go over
here to the overview.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:24.000
If I were to hit this
endpoint, this mashup endpoint,

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:26.520
what we should see is
both of those functions

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:29.583
being called one by one.

00:27:29.583 --> 00:27:31.000
And we should
compose the response

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:33.050
with both of their runtimes.

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:34.360
So you can see that there now.

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:35.620
So even with the
cold starts, you

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:37.245
can see the cold
starts are pretty fast

00:27:37.245 --> 00:27:39.850
and both Cloud Run
and Cloud Functions.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:41.830
And now, Apigee is
composing the response

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:44.450
from both of those
independent service calls

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:46.970
into something that I want
to do for the end user.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.420
So it gives me the ability
to compose and do whatever

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:51.058
I want without additional code.

00:27:51.058 --> 00:27:53.350
Now, there's one more thing
that we wanted to show you.

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:55.210
Where's the multi-cloud piece?

00:27:55.210 --> 00:27:57.170
I typically don't say
things like multi-cloud.

00:27:57.170 --> 00:27:59.380
There's multiple
environments where you

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:01.270
want to do some computation.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:03.400
The hard parts about
multi-cloud-- or multiple

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:05.500
environments or multiple
cloud providers--

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:08.657
is composing the network
layer and the storage layer.

00:28:08.657 --> 00:28:11.240
So what we'll see people do is
they'll just use best of breed.

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:12.910
So if you're using Amazon--

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.190
maybe you have a lot
of data in DynamoDB,

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.140
you're probably just
going to use Lambda.

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:19.420
If you have some stuff inside of
Google Cloud or cloud storage,

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:20.320
you're probably
just going to use

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:22.000
Cloud Run or Cloud Functions.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:23.860
We're not forcing you to choose.

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:26.110
So we've also built an
extension for Lambda,

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.995
very similar to the other
centers that I had before.

00:28:28.995 --> 00:28:30.370
When you write
your Lambda code--

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:32.410
if you've not written
any Lambda, good for you.

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:34.202
You're probably only
using Google services.

00:28:34.202 --> 00:28:35.754
That's what we want you to do.

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:42.220
But in the Lambda world, here's
another Go app, very similar.

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:43.350
You write a function main.

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.730
You give it a handler,
and it's going to respond.

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:48.120
Now, the response is a little
bit different in the Lambda

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:50.560
world, and you'll see when
I munge them altogether.

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:52.170
But what we want
Apigee to do is also

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:54.570
compose this multiple
cloud thing for us.

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.550
So Apigee is our
central control plane.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:58.990
It's our central entry point
for all of our clients,

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:02.490
but it has the ability to go
out to all the other endpoints

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:04.330
and aggregate all the responses.

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:06.490
So this is what we
mean by API management.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:09.107
We don't have to leak all of
these details to our end users.

00:29:09.107 --> 00:29:11.190
If you're in the organization
and some people want

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:13.560
to use one cloud provider,
and another team wants

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:15.100
to use a different
cloud provider,

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:16.475
you don't have to
argue too much.

00:29:16.475 --> 00:29:19.252
You can normalize that
stuff on the Edge.

00:29:19.252 --> 00:29:20.460
So we go over here to Lambda.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.310
I'm going to show you
that I actually have

00:29:22.310 --> 00:29:24.180
it deployed to Amazon's Lambda.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:26.230
So it's a standard.

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:27.820
It's also called Runtime.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:28.820
And then what we do is--

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.570
I'm going to show you the last
piece here in Apigee, where

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:32.790
we go to a different proxy.

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.070
Now, I could have done this
all in one single proxy,

00:29:35.070 --> 00:29:37.530
but I broke it up, so you
can actually see the steps.

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:39.915
This particular path
is called multi-cloud.

00:29:39.915 --> 00:29:41.580
So if I click on
multi-cloud, you'll

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.800
see the various calls
that I'm doing here.

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:47.360
I want to show you that
configuration here.

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:49.250
And you'll see-- hey,
don't be giving me

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:52.140
any errors in front of folks.

00:29:52.140 --> 00:29:55.020
So the first one here, I'm
calling out to that Cloud

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:56.190
Function like we had before.

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:02.710
Then here, we're going to
be calling out to the Lambda

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:04.670
using the Lambda extension.

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:06.670
And the nice thing about
this Lambda extension--

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:08.410
just like I said up
before with Cloud Run--

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:09.910
I just give it a
set of credentials.

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:10.812
So you go to Amazon.

00:30:10.812 --> 00:30:13.270
You create a set of credentials
that give it limited access

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:14.560
to invoke the Lambda function.

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.007
You don't need anything else.

00:30:16.007 --> 00:30:17.590
And then I can refer
to that extension

00:30:17.590 --> 00:30:20.440
here, and tell it very simply
the name of the function

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:21.400
is Runtime.

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:23.755
This is going to be a
synchronous request response.

00:30:23.755 --> 00:30:25.630
And then I'm going to
take that response that

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:28.797
comes back from Lambda, and
hold it in this variable.

00:30:28.797 --> 00:30:30.880
And again, just like I did
with a Cloud Functions,

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:32.800
I can take that
variable and then

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:36.910
use it as part of that
pipeline to munge the response.

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:41.966
So the only thing I'm doing
here is taking that response.

00:30:41.966 --> 00:30:42.892
Scroll up.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:50.130
I'm taking that response, and
I'm adding the Lambda response

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:50.630
to it.

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:52.750
So we should see an
additional response

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:54.560
in our particular array.

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:55.060
All right.

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:56.685
So let's go ahead
and hit this endpoint

00:30:56.685 --> 00:30:59.700
to verify that everything works.

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:02.430
So we'll click on
this overview here.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:04.350
And then we'll
grab this endpoint

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.220
to see if all three
can be invoked.

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:09.000
And we should get
that munge response.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:10.690
Click here.

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:12.290
And we'll go back
to our browser.

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:14.020
So you see that
there's two there now.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.700
We'll hit the
multi-cloud endpoint.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:19.390
You can see now we've added
the response that comes back

00:31:19.390 --> 00:31:22.390
from Amazon Lambda
as well using Apigee

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:23.800
to sit on top of all of those.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:26.200
And it gives you choice
between any cloud provider,

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:28.212
any serverless stack that
you would like to use.

00:31:28.212 --> 00:31:30.170
With that, I'd like to
end the particular demo.

00:31:30.170 --> 00:31:30.670
Thank you.

00:31:37.895 --> 00:31:39.270
PRITHPAL BHOGILL:
Thanks, Kelsey.

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:42.670
That was awesome.

00:31:42.670 --> 00:31:43.300
You guys think?

00:31:43.300 --> 00:31:44.272
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:44.272 --> 00:31:45.244
Awesome.

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:51.720
So what you saw
Kelsey take us through

00:31:51.720 --> 00:31:53.880
was something very powerful.

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:56.195
He mentioned a few
key capabilities

00:31:56.195 --> 00:31:57.570
that we built
around what we call

00:31:57.570 --> 00:32:01.650
it Apigee extensions, which
give you the ability to able--

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:03.210
it gives you the
ability to access

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:07.560
many cloud services, of course,
from Google Cloud Platform

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:11.510
and also from other
cloud providers as well.

00:32:11.510 --> 00:32:13.860
So Apigee API
management platform

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:16.510
is a full lifecycle
API platform,

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:18.910
which is a key
component when you start

00:32:18.910 --> 00:32:21.520
building your serverless
applications to ensure

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:24.370
that they are access
securely, they

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:28.810
have the right level
of controls in place,

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:31.435
and you have the right level of
operational visibility into it.

00:32:31.435 --> 00:32:33.393
So if you start looking
at the different layers

00:32:33.393 --> 00:32:35.590
of the platform, it starts
with the API runtime.

00:32:35.590 --> 00:32:38.590
What Kelsey was demonstrating
today was the core enterprise

00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:41.980
gateway and a set of
out-of-the-box policies,

00:32:41.980 --> 00:32:45.280
which make it very easy to
take data from different kinds

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:48.730
of serverless apps, do some
composition, as you just saw.

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:51.430
The next level up is known
as the mediation engine.

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:53.970
And that provides some
of the core capability

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:57.220
around taking and orchestrating
data from different sources.

00:32:57.220 --> 00:32:59.680
In this case, in
particular, Kelsey

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:02.440
demonstrated three different
types of extensions.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:05.740
So two of the extensions were
actually just announced today

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:09.820
at this session, the Google
Authentication extension

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:11.890
and the Cloud
Function extensions,

00:33:11.890 --> 00:33:14.660
which became available
generally today.

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:16.930
So those extensions
were a perfect example

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:21.040
of how you can take capabilities
from different serverless apps,

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:22.000
compose them.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.540
Well, he took it a step further.

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:25.970
He also showcased the
Lambda extension--

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:28.220
which is available
within Apigee as well--

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:32.080
which you can use to go access
your serverless capability

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:33.940
that you're building on AWS.

00:33:33.940 --> 00:33:35.620
As you go higher
up in the stack,

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:37.120
some area we didn't
cover today--

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:38.950
And I'll show some
screenshots real quick--

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:42.890
is the API monitoring and
analytics capabilities.

00:33:42.890 --> 00:33:45.700
They become very
important in the realm

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:49.270
of once you have an API
program, which is driving--

00:33:49.270 --> 00:33:51.490
and many of your
consumers are using API--

00:33:51.490 --> 00:33:53.080
you want to be
able to ensure you

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.870
have the right level
of visibility, not

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:58.030
only from an operational
perspective--

00:33:58.030 --> 00:33:59.610
of course, taking
care of things like

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:02.740
are my APIs performing well?

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:04.960
Do they have a lot
of errors suddenly?

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:06.190
Those kind of things--

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:09.550
to business metrics
around adoption.

00:34:09.550 --> 00:34:11.510
I spoke about great,
you're building

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:12.699
serverless applications.

00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:13.989
Who's using them?

00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:16.690
Do you have the right level
of metrics on adoption?

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:20.230
And if not, some corrective
actions could be taken.

00:34:20.230 --> 00:34:22.120
And the developer
ecosystem really

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:27.400
touches on the next level of how
do I really get the developers

00:34:27.400 --> 00:34:29.170
access to these kind
of applications--

00:34:29.170 --> 00:34:31.179
which are nothing
but API endpoints--

00:34:31.179 --> 00:34:32.860
at some point in time?

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:34.540
So I'll just walk
you through a couple

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:35.957
of slides here real quick.

00:34:35.957 --> 00:34:37.540
So this is an example
of the developer

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:41.260
portal, which is a key part of
the API management platform.

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:44.980
Using the constructs available
here, some of the API endpoints

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:49.120
that Kelsey was demonstrating
they weaken very quickly

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:53.500
and automatically generate
API documentation, which

00:34:53.500 --> 00:34:56.380
can be rendered on a
developer portal that any

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:58.750
of the application developer--

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:01.330
who are the consumers of
this so it lists capability

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:03.700
that you're building--
can very quickly access.

00:35:03.700 --> 00:35:05.180
They can try them out.

00:35:05.180 --> 00:35:07.030
And more importantly,
they can register,

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:08.740
start using those capabilities.

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:11.590
So Developer Portal
becomes a key entry point

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:15.750
for the consumers of the
serverless applications.

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:18.240
And the last I'll going
to touch a little bit on,

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:22.990
the consumption aspect of it,
and monitoring and analytics.

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:27.000
So these dashboards give you
a very high-level entry point

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:30.930
into the different kinds
of metrics the API platform

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:32.610
captures out-of-the-box.

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:34.890
So just as you saw Kelsey
walking through three

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:37.530
different API proxies,
the moment API traffic

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:40.290
starts to bite through
the API proxies,

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:43.350
we collect over 200
different data points,

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:46.260
and have aggregations
of data sets, which

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:51.100
can be accessed for APIs, or
also access through the UI--

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:53.490
which Kelsey showed
some parts of it today.

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:57.250
So one example is the
dashboard on the top left.

00:35:57.250 --> 00:35:59.160
We showed you the
overall engagement,

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:01.230
the overall traffic pattern.

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:03.270
What kind of API
calls are being made?

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:04.740
How many of them are in error?

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:05.972
So on and so forth.

00:36:05.972 --> 00:36:08.430
And then you start to see some
other aspects of performance

00:36:08.430 --> 00:36:13.130
latency analysis, and then the
ability to do custom reporting.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:16.440
If none of the 20 or so
out-of-the-box dashboard's not

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:19.140
enough, you can actually go
build your own custom reports.

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:20.640
And this is a very
important feature

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:26.070
because many times APIs, once
they become front and center

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:29.280
of how you solve data,
they're different stakeholders

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:32.238
in the API program, such
as line of businesses.

00:36:32.238 --> 00:36:33.780
If you're building
an order API, they

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:35.697
want to be able to know,
well, how many orders

00:36:35.697 --> 00:36:36.870
are passing through--

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:40.740
office-specific amount--
for a specific region?

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:42.580
You don't have to go
out of the platform.

00:36:42.580 --> 00:36:45.780
You can use custom report to
be able to just quickly report

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:49.230
that report, and give it to
the specific line of business

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:51.720
stakeholder who wants
it, versus having

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:54.990
to rely on getting data out,
using yet another platform

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:56.130
to build that out.

00:36:56.130 --> 00:36:57.750
OK.

00:36:57.750 --> 00:37:01.660
So with that, let's kind of
have Kelsey back over here.

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:03.930
Let's kind of summarize
some of the key takeaways.

00:37:03.930 --> 00:37:06.130
And then we'll take any
questions after this.

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:09.770
So if you want to
come on over, Kelsey.

00:37:09.770 --> 00:37:12.260
So just three high
level key takeaways.

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:15.410
I think we went through
a lot of things today,

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:18.890
but very quickly,
we spoke about some

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:21.830
of the serverless capabilities
that many of our customers

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:22.440
are adopting.

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:25.117
So clearly, it's very popular.

00:37:25.117 --> 00:37:27.200
Do you want to talk a
little bit about multi-cloud

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:28.410
and your experience?

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:29.480
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah,
so I think for me,

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:31.220
the serverless piece
is probably not

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:33.500
going to be one provider
platform specific.

00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:36.210
I look at them as ways
to program in the cloud.

00:37:36.210 --> 00:37:37.880
Sometimes you're
responding to events.

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:39.627
Something got
uploaded to a bucket.

00:37:39.627 --> 00:37:41.210
Sometimes you just
have business logic

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:42.835
that you've had for
years, and you just

00:37:42.835 --> 00:37:44.270
wanted to deploy
it via container.

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:45.660
And I decouple the problem.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:48.950
So as much as I like these
fully integrated platforms,

00:37:48.950 --> 00:37:50.750
I do like the ability
to compose, right?

00:37:50.750 --> 00:37:52.100
I have a Unix background.

00:37:52.100 --> 00:37:54.670
I would like to use an API
gateway for a certain thing.

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:57.110
I like to use different
runtimes for different things.

00:37:57.110 --> 00:37:59.660
And what we talked about today
is the ability to compose.

00:37:59.660 --> 00:38:01.743
What you saw in my demo
also is that I didn't have

00:38:01.743 --> 00:38:03.290
to use Amazon's API gateway.

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:05.725
I didn't have to use a
load balancer in GCP.

00:38:05.725 --> 00:38:07.100
And I was able to
pick and choose

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:09.107
how I wanted to orchestrate
my particular stack.

00:38:09.107 --> 00:38:10.190
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Perfect.

00:38:10.190 --> 00:38:12.620
And then last but not the
least, as you hopefully

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:14.840
will have realized
today, API management

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:16.010
is a very vital function.

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:19.280
When you're building serverless
applications, the moment you

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:21.380
expand the reach of those
serverless applications

00:38:21.380 --> 00:38:24.950
to your consumers, you want
to ensure they're secured.

00:38:24.950 --> 00:38:28.730
Only the right level of
authorization is allowed.

00:38:28.730 --> 00:38:31.650
In terms of access, you want
to have the right visibility.

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:34.098
So API management becomes
a very critical function

00:38:34.098 --> 00:38:36.140
as you start to roll out
more and more serverless

00:38:36.140 --> 00:38:38.290
applications.

